WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.095
Picture a café owner at closing time.

2
00:00:05.095 --> 00:00:11.439
A customer's coffee was charged twice, so she opens the payments app on her phone and taps Refund.

3
00:00:11.633 --> 00:00:13.357
The app says, refund sent.

4
00:00:13.357 --> 00:00:17.599
She reloads the page to check, and the refund isn't in the list.

5
00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:19.490
So she taps Refund again.

6
00:00:19.490 --> 00:00:21.383
Did the first refund vanish?

7
00:00:21.383 --> 00:00:24.222
And if it didn't, why couldn't she see it?

8
00:00:24.222 --> 00:00:25.507
Hold that question.

9
00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:27.392
Picture a club's notebook.

10
00:00:27.392 --> 00:00:30.973
One clerk writes each change on the next numbered line.

11
00:00:30.973 --> 00:00:37.027
Copies are posted to other towns, and each town's copy is right as of the last letter it got.

12
00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.406
The payments app works the same way.

13
00:00:39.406 --> 00:00:45.903
One computer, the leader, writes every change at the next position in its log, a numbered list of changes.

14
00:00:45.903 --> 00:00:50.255
Other computers, the followers, copy that log in order, a little later.

15
00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:55.424
Most pages read from a follower, so the leader keeps its time for card payments.

16
00:00:55.424 --> 00:00:59.793
And a read on a follower shows the data as of that follower's position.

17
00:00:60.000 --> 00:01:02.703
Now let's put a clock on her evening.

18
00:01:02.703 --> 00:01:05.990
At 1,000 ms, she asks for a refund of pay-41.

19
00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:10.153
The leader commits it, writes it for good, at position 2.

20
00:01:10.333 --> 00:01:12.649
At 1,010 ms, her page reloads.

21
00:01:12.649 --> 00:01:20.523
A load balancer, a program that spreads reads across the followers, sends the question to follower r1.

22
00:01:20.733 --> 00:01:22.457
r1 is 300 ms behind.

23
00:01:22.457 --> 00:01:24.611
It's still at position 0.

24
00:01:24.611 --> 00:01:29.351
So the refund list is empty, and she taps Refund again.

25
00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:34.296
The database turns the second refund away as a repeat, so no money is lost.

26
00:01:34.296 --> 00:01:38.580
But the screen was wrong, and her call to the support line is real.

27
00:01:38.767 --> 00:01:40.948
Her refund was safe the whole time.

28
00:01:40.948 --> 00:01:42.444
It was only in the post.

29
00:01:42.444 --> 00:01:47.555
So the real question is: how can a page tell whether this copy has her change yet?

30
00:01:47.767 --> 00:01:50.993
You might be thinking: send her reads to the leader.

31
00:01:50.993 --> 00:01:51.924
That's correct.

32
00:01:51.924 --> 00:01:57.323
But most pages show a shop owner's own data, so soon the leader takes most reads again.

33
00:01:58.100 --> 00:01:59.524
Here's a cleverer one.

34
00:01:59.524 --> 00:02:03.732
For one minute after each change she makes, read from the leader.

35
00:02:03.732 --> 00:02:06.516
After that, a follower is surely caught up.

36
00:02:06.516 --> 00:02:07.487
Will this hold?

37
00:02:07.487 --> 00:02:08.523
Pause and guess.

38
00:02:11.767 --> 00:02:17.256
It passes every test, because on a quiet test computer, lag is a few milliseconds.

39
00:02:17.467 --> 00:02:21.694
Then one night a follower restarts and spends six minutes catching up.

40
00:02:21.694 --> 00:02:26.163
For every owner who changed something in that window, the change vanishes.

41
00:02:26.333 --> 00:02:28.768
One minute was a guess about the clock.

42
00:02:28.768 --> 00:02:31.701
A timer is a promise the follower never signed.

43
00:02:32.167 --> 00:02:33.209
Back to the club.

44
00:02:33.209 --> 00:02:38.117
When you send the clerk a change, she writes it on line 2 and tells you: line 2.

45
00:02:38.117 --> 00:02:40.571
You write that on the back of your hand.

46
00:02:40.767 --> 00:02:43.756
Later you walk into a town's reading room.

47
00:02:43.756 --> 00:02:46.104
First, you look at its last line.

48
00:02:46.104 --> 00:02:46.603
Line 0?

49
00:02:46.603 --> 00:02:48.524
Your change hasn't arrived.

50
00:02:48.524 --> 00:02:52.011
Wait a little, or go to the clerk's own notebook.

51
00:02:52.011 --> 00:02:53.150
Line 2 or later?

52
00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:54.360
Read right there.

53
00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:55.229
Now map it.

54
00:02:55.229 --> 00:02:57.613
The line number is the log position.

55
00:02:57.613 --> 00:03:03.971
The number on your hand is a token, kept in her session, the app's memory of one signed-in user.

56
00:03:04.167 --> 00:03:10.787
And whoever checks the last line before you read is the router, the part of the app that picks a machine for each read.

57
00:03:10.967 --> 00:03:13.844
Let's replay her evening with the token.

58
00:03:13.844 --> 00:03:20.029
At 1,000 ms, the leader commits the refund at position 2 and sends that position back.

59
00:03:20.029 --> 00:03:22.187
Her session keeps it: token 2.

60
00:03:22.333 --> 00:03:25.067
At 1,010 ms, the page reloads.

61
00:03:25.067 --> 00:03:28.348
The router asks r1 for its position.

62
00:03:28.348 --> 00:03:29.260
r1 says 0.

63
00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:31.447
Where does this read go?

64
00:03:31.447 --> 00:03:32.905
Pause and guess.

65
00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:39.416
0 is below 2, so the read goes to the leader.

66
00:03:39.416 --> 00:03:40.963
The refund is listed.

67
00:03:40.963 --> 00:03:42.584
She doesn't tap again.

68
00:03:43.067 --> 00:03:46.241
At 1,300 ms, r1 has applied position 2.

69
00:03:46.241 --> 00:03:50.312
Now 2 is at or past 2, so her reads go back to r1.

70
00:03:50.312 --> 00:03:55.033
The leader only took the reads that would have been wrong.

71
00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:57.736
This promise has a name: read-your-writes.

72
00:03:57.736 --> 00:04:01.720
After you change something, your own reads always show the change.

73
00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:04.021
Notice what it doesn't promise.

74
00:04:04.021 --> 00:04:07.306
A support agent who changed nothing has token 0.

75
00:04:07.306 --> 00:04:11.823
Every follower qualifies, so the agent may not see the refund yet.

76
00:04:11.823 --> 00:04:12.849
That's allowed.

77
00:04:14.133 --> 00:04:16.027
Two costs come with the token.

78
00:04:16.027 --> 00:04:20.067
First, it must live where every one of her requests can find it.

79
00:04:20.067 --> 00:04:25.055
A cookie, the small note a website keeps in one browser, works for one browser.

80
00:04:25.233 --> 00:04:28.517
But she refunds on her phone and checks on her laptop.

81
00:04:28.517 --> 00:04:31.497
So the server keeps the token, under her account.

82
00:04:31.667 --> 00:04:35.654
Second, a follower that's paused or broken may never catch up.

83
00:04:35.654 --> 00:04:37.583
Don't let a read wait forever.

84
00:04:37.583 --> 00:04:40.220
Wait a short time, then go to the leader.

85
00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:45.398
There's one more option: make every commit wait until a follower has applied it.

86
00:04:45.398 --> 00:04:48.584
Every read is fresh, and every payment pays for it.

87
00:04:48.767 --> 00:04:55.511
On PostgreSQL, a free database program, the token is the log position, read right after the commit.

88
00:04:55.511 --> 00:04:58.508
The check on the follower is one comparison.

89
00:05:00.367 --> 00:05:02.559
So, did her first refund vanish?

90
00:05:02.559 --> 00:05:02.765
No.

91
00:05:02.765 --> 00:05:04.889
It was on the leader all along.

92
00:05:04.889 --> 00:05:07.561
She read a copy that hadn't reached it.

93
00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:12.769
The fix is to carry her last write's position, and refuse to read below it.

94
00:05:12.769 --> 00:05:14.796
Compare positions, not clocks.

95
00:05:15.367 --> 00:05:16.772
One quick check.

96
00:05:16.772 --> 00:05:18.090
Her token is 5.

97
00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:20.638
Follower r2 is at position 7.

98
00:05:20.638 --> 00:05:22.570
Where can her read go?

99
00:05:22.570 --> 00:05:23.976
Pause and think.

100
00:05:27.167 --> 00:05:27.708
To r2.

101
00:05:27.708 --> 00:05:31.681
7 is past 5, so her change is already there.
